{
  "dom_id": "question-133234",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Let $r(\\theta )=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\theta \\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ and let ${P}_{2}(\\theta )$ be the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $r(\\theta )$ about $\\theta =0.$ Use the Lagrange error bound to find an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when ${P}_{2}(\\theta )$ is used to approximate $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.2).$ Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $|{R}_{2}|\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The Lagrange error bound states that an upper bound for the error ${R}_{2}$ obtained when using the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}$ about $\\theta =a$ to approximate $r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\n|{R}_{2}|\\le \\frac{M}{3!}|\\theta -a{|}^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the maximum value of $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|$ on the interval between $a=0$ and $\\theta =0.2.$\n\nPlugging $\\theta =0.2$ and $a=0$ into the above formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{M}{6}|0.2{|}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find $M,$ we need to maximize $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|$ on the interval $[0,0.2].$ Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )=-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that for $\\theta \\in [0,0.2],$ we have\n\n$$\n|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|=|-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta |=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $|{r}^{(3)}(\\theta )|=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is a decreasing function on the interval $[0,0.2],$ its value is greatest at $\\theta =0.$ So,\n\n$$\nM=|{r}^{(3)}(0)|=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the upper bound is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{R}_{2}| & \\le \\frac{6}{6}|0.2{|}^{3} \\\\  & =(0.2)^{3} \\\\  & =0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
